} (step_04).\n- A **static** 2-hop path does exist: 158 reaches both 639 and 704 (step_06), and 189 reaches 704 (step_07); 1736's out-set {144, 279, 319, 470, 682, 1151, 1194, 1249, 1688, 1937} is disjoint from 883's in-set (step_08). So any candidate route is 1010\u2192158\u2192{639|704}\u2192883 or 1010\u2192189\u2192704\u2192883.\n- Crucially, **none of the available tools expose timestamps of individual events**. The k-hop expansion (step_05) returns edges without times. So I can confirm untimed reachability, but I cannot verify (a) that these specific hops fall inside [51317, 89717], (b) that they occur in increasing order, or (c) that consecutive gaps are \u2264 600 time units. Node 158 is a hub with 363 out-neighbours (step_06), which is exactly the confounder the task warns about: hub-mediated static connectivity easily fabricates paths that are not time-respecting, and with a 600-unit gap budget against a 38,400-unit window, the prior that a randomly-placed hub edge chains correctly is low.\n\nBecause the decisive temporal ordering evidence is unobtainable with the instruments used, I decline to assert a cascade.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_20982933\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1010, 883, 158, 189, 1736, 639, 704],\n  \"window\": [51317, 89717],\n  \"confidence\": 0.48,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Untimed reachability from 1010 to 883 exists via exactly three candidate 2-hop routes (1010->158->639->883, 1010->158->704->883, 1010->189->704->883); 1736 is a dead end for 883. However, the only intermediary carrying most of this connectivity is node 158, a hub with 363 out-neighbours, so static reachability here is a weak signal and is explicitly the confounder to be controlled. No tool in this session returned event timestamps, so I could not verify that the hops lie within [51317, 89717], occur in increasing time order, or respect the 600-unit maximum inter-hop delay. With 1010 having only 6 total events and the graph being nearly repeat-free, a single mistimed hop would break the chain. I therefore report insufficient temporal evidence rather than inferring a cascade from static connectivity.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 14553, "total_output_tokens": 2733, "total_tokens": 17286, "total_latency_ms": 41234, "llm_calls": 4, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}